**2. Wire it up in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Register Problem Details services
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ExceptionToProblemDetailsHandler>();

var app = builder.Build();

// Must be early in the pipeline
app.UseExceptionHandler();

// Your endpoints
app.MapGet("/items/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    if (id < 0) throw new ArgumentException("ID must be positive.");
    if (id == 0) throw new KeyNotFoundException("Item not found.");
    if (id == 99) throw new InvalidOperationException("Item is archived.");
    return Results.Ok(new { Id = id, Name = "Widget" });
});

app.Run();
```

**How it works:**

- `IExceptionHandler` (introduced in .NET 8) intercepts all unhandled exceptions globally — no per-endpoint try-catch needed.
- The switch expression maps exception types → HTTP status codes.
- For 500s, the actual exception message is suppressed; only a generic detail is returned.
- Responses use `application/json` with the standard Problem Details shape (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`).

**Example 404 response:**
```json
{
  "status": 404,
  "title": "Resource Not Found",
  "detail": "Item not found.",
  "instance": "/items/0"
}
```
